From 020ca8448d3f866835861961e570233d7a0dda12 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 09:08:55 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 078/119] scene-referred S3: the boundary audit - who reads
 what, on which side of the conversion

No behaviour change. The conversion point is only correct if nothing downstream of
it expects Rec.709 and nothing upstream of it has already been converted, and that
is a claim about pass ORDER that has to be walked rather than assumed. It was
walked, in CStandardGraphicsPipeline::Execute() order, and the result is recorded
both in scene-notes/STRUCTURE.md section 7b (the full table) and as comments at the
three sites in the code where the next person will actually be standing.

The finding that mattered: the conversion runs inside ExecuteHDRPostProcessing(),
called at :562, and EVERY forward and transparent pass - transparents below water,
the water stage, transparents above water, the depth fixup and the half-resolution
particles - is at :526 to :537. They have all finished. No forward blend ever lands
709 colour in an AP1 target, and refraction is safe for a second reason as well:
CSceneRenderPass::ResolvePass() re-fills $SceneTarget from the still-709 HDR target
immediately before each refractive draw.

The three readers the whole design turns on - SSR (:446), the water reflection
(:528) and the SVOGI previous-frame bounce, inside lighting - all bind
$HDRTargetPrev, all run before :562, and all therefore read the Rec.709 frame the
copy handed them. Nothing had to be reordered and no reader needed a conversion of
its own.

Sites now carrying a comment saying which space they are in and why:
  CSnowStage::Execute()          the ONE post-conversion writer of 709-authored
                                 colour. Near-achromatic, small error, left to break
                                 visibly per D13. S8 list. (CRainStage and the
                                 deferred snow displacement are both on the correct
                                 side, which is worth saying because it looks
                                 accidental.)
  CLensOpticsStage::Execute()    not a consumer of the working space at all - it
                                 OVERWRITES $SceneTargetR11G11B10F[0] from the flare
                                 textures without reading it, and the result is
                                 composited in the LDR PostAA stage after the tone
                                 map. Display-referred on both paths; it becomes
                                 D9's problem in S4, not S3's.
  CDepthOfFieldStage's copy      $HDRTarget -> $SceneTarget is AP1 to AP1. It does
                                 leave $SceneTarget in AP1 for the rest of the
                                 frame, which only a shader reading it WITHOUT
                                 triggering a resolve would ever see - and that read
                                 was already stale in stock CE.

STRUCTURE.md section 7b also records what S3 deliberately did NOT fix, so none of it
gets mistaken for a defect later: the 709-authored CPU tints (anamorphic, halation,
the six-wavelength diffraction spectrum, the ghost tint and its hue rotation) now
multiply AP1 data and each wants its own visible commit; every absolute threshold
now compares against a different number, which is D11 / S7; r_HDRDebug 2 sRGB-encodes
AP1 through a 709 curve; the flip frame still leaves $HDRTargetPrev undefined for one
frame; and the tone map itself is now being fed AP1 values by a Hable curve and an
sRGB encode that both think they are looking at Rec.709 - which is the expected
intermediate state until S4 replaces them with the ODT.

Build: CryRenderD3D11, CinematicCamera, 0/0.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01A72MFkdSczncE7au9UVnmu
---
 .../XRenderD3D9/GraphicsPipeline/DepthOfField.cpp   | 10 ++++++++++
 .../GraphicsPipeline/StandardGraphicsPipeline.cpp   | 13 +++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 23 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Code/CryEngine/RenderDll/XRenderD3D9/GraphicsPipeline/DepthOfField.cpp b/Code/CryEngine/RenderDll/XRenderD3D9/GraphicsPipeline/DepthOfField.cpp
index 61dab438..2bac13a8 100644
--- a/Code/CryEngine/RenderDll/XRenderD3D9/GraphicsPipeline/DepthOfField.cpp
+++ b/Code/CryEngine/RenderDll/XRenderD3D9/GraphicsPipeline/DepthOfField.cpp
@@ -674,6 +674,16 @@ void CDepthOfFieldStage::Execute()
 	Vec4 vDofParams1 = Vec4(CRenderer::CV_r_dofMinZ + dofParams.vMinZParams.x, CRenderer::CV_r_dofMinZScale + dofParams.vMinZParams.y, fNearestDofScaleBoost, vFocus.w);
 
 	// For better blending later
+	//
+	// Scene-referred pipeline (SceneReferredSpec.md S3): both ends of this copy are ACEScg - the
+	// source is the already-converted HDR target and the only consumer is this stage's own
+	// composite. What it also does, on both paths, is leave $SceneTarget holding the LAST thing
+	// written to it for the rest of the frame. $SceneTarget is addressable by name from material
+	// shader scripts (ShaderTemplate.cpp) as the refraction source, but every refractive draw
+	// happens in the forward transparent block, which CSceneRenderPass::ResolvePass re-fills from
+	// the Rec.709 HDR target immediately before, and which finishes long before the conversion
+	// point. So refraction reads 709 on both paths; only a shader that read $SceneTarget WITHOUT
+	// triggering a resolve would see a stale frame, and that read was already stale in stock CE.
 	m_passCopySceneTarget.Execute(m_graphicsPipelineResources.m_pTexHDRTarget, m_graphicsPipelineResources.m_pTexSceneTarget);
 
 	static CCryNameR dofFocusParam0Name("vDofParamsFocus0");
diff --git a/Code/CryEngine/RenderDll/XRenderD3D9/GraphicsPipeline/StandardGraphicsPipeline.cpp b/Code/CryEngine/RenderDll/XRenderD3D9/GraphicsPipeline/StandardGraphicsPipeline.cpp
index 0ba6340a..737b3bee 100644
--- a/Code/CryEngine/RenderDll/XRenderD3D9/GraphicsPipeline/StandardGraphicsPipeline.cpp
+++ b/Code/CryEngine/RenderDll/XRenderD3D9/GraphicsPipeline/StandardGraphicsPipeline.cpp
@@ -284,6 +284,12 @@ void CStandardGraphicsPipeline::ExecuteHDRPostProcessing()
 	if (GetStage<CMotionBlurStage>()->IsStageActive(m_renderingFlags))
 		GetStage<CMotionBlurStage>()->Execute();
 
+	// Scene-referred pipeline: this is the ONE writer that lands on the far side of the
+	// working-space conversion with Rec.709-authored colour. Snow is near-achromatic, so the
+	// error is small, and per SceneReferredSpec.md D13 it is left to break visibly rather than
+	// switched off. (CRainStage above sits on the correct side: it writes before the copy, so it
+	// is converted along with everything else. CSnowStage::ExecuteDeferredSnowDisplacement()
+	// likewise runs before ExecuteHDRPostProcessing().) S8 breakage list.
 	if (GetStage<CSnowStage>()->IsStageActive(m_renderingFlags))
 		GetStage<CSnowStage>()->Execute();
 
@@ -339,6 +345,13 @@ void CStandardGraphicsPipeline::ExecuteHDRPostProcessing()
 	GetStage<CDepthOfFieldStage>()->ExecuteGhostComposite();
 
 	// writes m_graphicsPipelineResources.m_pTexSceneTargetR11G11B10F[0]
+	//
+	// Scene-referred pipeline: this is NOT a consumer of the working space, despite writing a
+	// target that motion blur filled with ACEScg a few passes ago - it overwrites it wholesale
+	// from the flare textures and never reads it. The result is composited in the LDR PostAA
+	// stage, i.e. after the tone map, so the flares stay display-referred on both paths and the
+	// primaries change cannot reach them. (They become D9's problem in S4, when the whole stock
+	// LDR stage is skipped under the camera, not S3's.)
 	if (GetStage<CLensOpticsStage>()->IsStageActive(m_renderingFlags))
 		GetStage<CLensOpticsStage>()->Execute();
 
